---
title: "The block model & protocol"
description: "The document as a list of identified blocks: the content-hash contract, a diff with no Move op, and click-to-source."
---

In Taliesin the document is a list of **blocks**,
each of which carries its identity and its origin. Our `post.tmd` is three of them,
the `# Cooling coffee` heading, the paragraph, and the Python cell, plus a fourth
the cell *produces*: the plot, a derived block keyed to the cell.

## The block model

Every top-level element is a block. Each block carries three data attributes that
the rest of the system keys off:

- `data-block-id` is a **content hash** (with a positional tiebreak for
  duplicates). Because it is content-derived, an unchanged block keeps its id
  across edits and is never touched.
- `data-sourcepos` is the `startLine:startCol-endLine:endCol` range the block came
  from. Click-to-source uses it in both directions.
- `data-source-file` is present only on blocks pulled in from an
  `{{< include >}}`d file, so cross-file source mapping still works.

Source mapping, incremental re-render, and live-state preservation all build on
this model. A code cell's *output* is itself a block, keyed to the cell's id,
so when an upstream cell re-runs, only the affected output blocks update.

The exact field shapes (`Block`, `Cell`, `RenderedDoc`, and friends) live in
`crates/core/src/render/model.rs`, one small file with a doc comment per field.

### How the id is computed

`make_id` (`render/mod.rs`) is small and deterministic, so two renders of the same
source always agree and the diff has a stable anchor:

1. Take the block's **raw Markdown source**, trimmed of leading and trailing
   whitespace.
2. Hash it with **64-bit FNV-1a** (offset basis `0xcbf29ce484222325`, prime
   `0x100000001b3`), the same hash the freeze cache uses for cell keys.
3. Format the digest as 16 hex digits, keep the **first 12**, and prefix `b-`. So
   an id looks like `b-3af19c0b7e21`.
4. If a byte-identical block already produced that base id, append a positional
   `-N` suffix (the first duplicate becomes `b-…-1`, the next `b-…-2`, and so on),
   via `dedup_with_suffix`. This is the only place position enters the id, and
   only to break exact-content ties, so ids stay unique within a document.

Position stays out of the hash so that inserting a paragraph at the top of a
document leaves every following block's id unchanged, and the diff sees one insert
rather than "everything moved". The `-N` tiebreak lets the diff treat ids as
*unique* (the basis for the `O(n log n)` shortcut below).

A **generated** block has no source text of its own (the synthesized title header, the
References block, the footnotes section), so it skips the hash entirely and takes a fixed
id with an empty `data-sourcepos`.

## The diff (`diff.rs`)

`diff_blocks(old, new)` turns two block lists into the minimal set of DOM ops the
client applies. Because ids are content hashes, an unchanged block has the *same*
id in both lists, so the algorithm is an **LCS over the id sequences**:

1. The LCS finds the matched id pairs: the **stable anchors** that didn't move.
2. The runs of blocks *between* consecutive anchors are the changed regions.
   `emit_gap` pairs them positionally: each pair → an **`Update`** (replace the old
   block's element with the new HTML); a surplus of old blocks → **`Remove`**; a
   surplus of new blocks → **`Insert`** after the preceding block's id (or at the
   start when there is none).
3. A matched anchor is normally left untouched. When its HTML *does* differ,
   `anchor_op` chooses between two outcomes by comparing the bodies with the
   source-position attributes masked out:
   - if only `data-sourcepos` moved (an edit *above* shifted the block's line numbers,
     but its content is byte-identical), it emits a **`SetMeta`**: patch the positions in
     place, with no re-render, so the live element keeps its DOM state;
   - if the body itself changed, it emits an **`Update`**. This is the path a
     **derived** block whose id isn't a pure content hash (a code cell's output block,
     keyed to the cell id) takes when an upstream cell re-runs.

### How `SetMeta` vs `Update` is decided

The "with the source-position attributes masked out" step compares the two htmls with
every `data-sourcepos` *attribute value* blanked and everything else required to match byte
for byte. A `data-source-file` that changed is therefore a real difference, and takes
`Update`.

A leaf block carries one position, on its own element, so the common case is a cheap
string comparison with the first value blanked. A `:::` div carries its inner blocks'
positions too, and a line shift above it moves all of them, so its `SetMeta` also carries
`inner`: the new value of every position inside it, in document order, which the client
writes onto the div's own descendants. Those values are read through the tag walker, so
prose that merely quotes `data-sourcepos="…"` inside a `<code>` span is content and must
match. A div that does not close is re-rendered instead, because the browser has moved the
blocks after it inside it and its descendants are no longer its own html's.

The whole decision is mechanical once the anchors are known (@fig-diff):

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-diff
%%| fig-cap: "How diff_blocks turns two block-id lists into DOM ops."
flowchart TD
  lists["old block ids vs new block ids"] --> lcs["LCS over the ids"]
  lcs --> anchor{"matched anchor?"}
  anchor -->|"HTML identical"| keep["leave untouched"]
  anchor -->|"only sourcepos moved"| sm["SetMeta"]
  anchor -->|"body changed (cell output)"| u1["Update"]
  lcs --> gap{"gap between anchors"}
  gap -->|"old / new pair"| u2["Update"]
  gap -->|"surplus old"| rem["Remove"]
  gap -->|"surplus new"| ins["Insert after prev id"]
```

### Why the diff is `O(n log n)`

The textbook LCS is an `O(m·n)` dynamic-programming table, and on a save it would
be reallocated on every keystroke: tens of megabytes once a document reaches a few
thousand blocks. Taliesin never builds it.

The shortcut is that block ids are unique within a document. A common
subsequence is then just a set of shared ids in the same relative order, so the
LCS collapses to a **Longest Increasing Subsequence**: map each old block to its
position in the new list, and the longest still-increasing run is the set of
anchors that didn't move. LIS is solved in `O(n log n)` time and `O(n)` space by
**patience sorting**, which is what `lcs_pairs` does. The cost of a diff is
therefore dominated by the handful of blocks that changed, not by the size of the
document.

An unchanged block is never touched, so the browser keeps its scroll position and
any live DOM state inside it (a running Three.js canvas, a `{js}` cell) survives a
save (goal #2).

Change `tau` and the diff emits one op, an `Update` for the plot's block, while the
heading and the paragraph, their content hashes unchanged, never move.

A real warm edit also shows the cost in bytes. Typing one new paragraph above an
already-open collapse callout, benchmarked by `tools/live-edit-bench`, emits 55 operations:
54 of them (every shifted paragraph, heading, figure and code cell below the edit, and the
open `::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}` div with the positions of its inner blocks) are
`SetMeta` patches like the one above, so anything live in those blocks keeps running and
the callout stays open. Together with the one `insert` for the new paragraph itself they
weigh about 3.2 KB, and each block's cost depends only on how many positions it carries.
(See [Choosing Taliesin](https://guide.taliesin.sh/using/choosing.html#sec-speed) for the
full measurement.)

::: {.callout-note}
## No Move op

Reordering two blocks keeps one as an anchor and rebuilds the other as
remove + insert, so a *moved* live block loses its runtime state.
Content-hash ids trade `Move` for simplicity (a pinned regression test
documents it); moves are rare in editing.
:::

### Removes before inserts (the ordering rule)

`diff_blocks` produces the ops anchor by anchor in document order, then applies a
stable sort that hoists every `Remove` ahead of every `Insert`. The client applies
the ops in the order it receives them, so its correctness depends on this ordering.

The reason is the no-`Move` case above. A block that moves *forward* is
emitted as a `Remove` at its old slot plus an `Insert` of the same id at its new
slot. If the `Insert` ran first, the new element (carrying that id) would already be
in the DOM when the `Remove` ran, and `Remove`'s id-based lookup would find and
delete the freshly inserted element instead of the stale one. Hoisting the
`Remove` makes the old element gone before the new one arrives, so the lookup is
unambiguous. Removes are positionally independent (each names a concrete
`target_id`), so reordering them is safe; the sort is *stable*, which keeps the
`Insert`s in document order so their `after_id` chains still resolve.

## The websocket protocol

On connect, the server sends a `full_render`; on each save it diffs the new block
list against the old one (an LCS over block ids) and broadcasts only the
differences. The exception is raw HTML the page does not hold as one element of its own:
a comment and a lone closing tag emit no element, and a wrapper's unclosed opening line
swallows the blocks after it. A save whose ops aim at, anchor on or bring in such a block
is sent as a `full_render` instead (`needs_remount` in `diff.rs`).

| Direction | Message | Payload |
|---|---|---|
| server → client | `full_render` | `title`, `body_html`, `diagnostics`, `gen`, `boot` |
| server → client | `update` | `target_id`, `html`, `gen` |
| server → client | `insert` | `after_id`, `html`, `gen` |
| server → client | `remove` | `target_id`, `gen` |
| server → client | `set_meta` | `target_id`, `sourcepos`, `source_file`, `inner`, `gen` (patch a shifted block's source mapping in place; `inner` holds a `:::` div's inner positions) |
| server → client | `title` | `title` (retitle the tab with no re-mount, when the front matter's title changed but the body did not) |
| server → client | `build-state` | `phase`, `ran`, `total`, `lang` (document-level execution progress, k of N; each page has its own socket, so no message names a page) |
| server → client | `cell-state` | `cell_id`, `state`, `started_ms`, `duration_ms`, `source` (`"cache"` or `"fresh"`: the queued / running / done badges, and the `⚡ cached` one that answers "why didn't my cell re-run?") |
| server → client | `cell-output-append` | `cell_id`, `op`, `html` (a preview of a still-running cell's output, so a long job shows its log rather than a bare `⏳`; the authoritative version still arrives as a block `update`, which is why `build` emits none of these) |
| server → client | `diagnostics` | `messages` (the page's diagnostics, each a `level`, `message`, `file` and, when known, `line` and a code `frame`) |
| server → client | `error` | `message` (a failed render; shown as an overlay) |
| server → client | `reload` | *(none)*; full page reload (config change, kernel restart) |
| client → server | `click_block` | `block_id`, `source_file`, `sourcepos` |
| client → server | `restart_kernel` | *(none)*; drop + respawn the kernel, re-run all cells |

These message shapes are defined once, in `protocol.rs`, and shared by the dev
server and the browser client. `serve_site/` and `web-client/` are the two ends of this
contract, so they can't drift apart from each other. The `update` / `insert` / `remove`
/ `set_meta` messages are just the serialized `BlockOp` enum (the variant name
lowercased into the `type` field); its four Rust variants and their fields are
declared in `crates/core/src/diff.rs`.
For the exact JSON shape of every message and when each is sent, read
`crates/server/src/protocol.rs`; for how the client applies one, `web-client/client.js`.

Two orderings matter to the live view. On a rebuild, each code cell the edit changed goes
out as an `update` of the block it replaces (and its old output as a `remove`) before any
cell runs: the edit changed the cell's id, and its `running` badge and live output are
addressed to the new one. And a `cell-state` of `running` sent again for a cell that is
already running empties its live output, which is how the server redraws it after
`clear_output` or an in-place display update, which `append` / `replace_last` cannot
express.

### Applying the ops (the client)

The browser side (`web-client/client.js`) is thin: each op is one small DOM
mutation, and the diff above keeps that set small.

- **`update`** finds the target by id (a child of `#tali-root` first, since ops name
  top-level blocks) and `replaceWith`s the new HTML; **`insert`**
  builds a fragment and places it after `after_id` (or `prepend`s it when there is
  none); **`remove`** drops the element; **`set_meta`** patches only `data-sourcepos` /
  `data-source-file` on the existing element (and the inner positions of a `:::` div on
  its descendants) and does not re-render. An op whose target or anchor is not in the
  page reloads it rather than guess, and a `<script>` an op brings in is re-created so it
  runs, as it would have on load.
- Every mutation runs inside **`keepScroll`**, which pins `window.scrollY` across the
  change (instantly, never animated), and is followed by **`afterChange`**, which
  rebuilds the TOC, re-runs the code enhancers, and re-scans for
  cell errors. `set_meta` skips `afterChange` (nothing rendered).
- The first paint is server-rendered into `#tali-root`, so the first `full_render`
  after connect is identical and is skipped, which avoids a flash and a needless
  `{js}` re-init. A *reconnect* re-mounts normally, and a dropped socket retries
  on a short loop.
- Because block ids are unique, an `insert` first drops any element already carrying
  the incoming id. The server emits all `remove`s before any `insert`s, so this is
  normally a no-op; it only matters if a reorder (a `remove` + `insert` of the same id)
  ever arrived out of order.

## Inverse search & forward search

Click-to-source (goal #1) uses only the two source attributes:

- **Inverse search (preview → editor).** Ctrl-clicking (Cmd-clicking on Mac) a block
  reads its `data-sourcepos` (+ `data-source-file`) and opens the exact source
  line: a `vscode://file/<path>:<line>:<col>` deep link by default. The source positions
  start as comrak's per-node ranges; on the way to becoming blocks they are
  translated through the include source map (`includes.rs` records a
  `LineOrigin` for every expanded line → its origin file + line), so a block that
  came from an `{{< include >}}`d partial carries that file in `data-source-file`
  and the *original* line, which lets click-to-source work across files.
- **Forward search (editor → preview).** The client also speaks a `postMessage` protocol
  as the integration surface for an embedded editor host. The in-repo [VS Code
  companion](extending.tmd) is that host: the client posts `tali-goto` up to it (which
  reveals the source), and accepts `tali-cursor {file, line, reveal}`. That last one maps
  a source line back to a block: `highlightAtLine` picks the smallest block whose
  `data-sourcepos` covers the line (else the nearest block starting before it) and
  outlines it with `.tali-hl`, scrolling it into view only when `reveal` is set. So an
  editor can host the preview and add cursor sync without forking the client.

Both directions only navigate and highlight: the `.tmd` file stays the single editing
surface, and nothing in the preview or the editor-client integration ever writes back to
it.
